  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Family: Amphiliidae. Size: 6 cm. Temperature: 18–22°C. pH: 6.5–7.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>This species and probably most of this genus can be easily misidentified, and the thumbnail images may show this species or a closely related species such as A. platychir or A. uranoscopus. They are all more or less the same when kept in aquaria. It is not imported very often but would make a good aquarium addition if kept in optimum conditions.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Gbin River, Guinea, and Pra River, from Sierra Leone to Ghana. Type locality: Atesu River, Gold Coast (Ghana); also occurs in Sierra Leone, Liberia and the Ivory Coast.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>The genus inhabits flowing waters in its natural habitat. The last two thumbnail images were photographed in Tai National Park in south-west Côte d'Ivoire, close to the border with Liberia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Can be kept in aquaria with a small-grained gravel or sand substrate, well planted, with a slight current to mimic their natural habitat. Provide rockwork and caves.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Water should be kept on the cool side, as this genus inhabits flowing waters. Temperature 18–22°C, pH 6.5–7.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Mosquito larvae, Tubifex, Cyclops, Daphnia, grindal worms, vegetable food, tablet and flake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the specific name atesuensis: –ensis, suffix denoting place: Atesu River, Ghana, type locality. IUCN Red List: widely distributed from the St. John (Liberia) to the Mono river (Togo), and in the upper Niger basin. It has no known widespread threats and is assessed as Least Concern (IUCN 2020).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_atesuensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Baensch, H.A. and R. Riehl 1991 Aquarien atlas. Bd. 3. Melle: Mergus, Verlag für Natur- und Heimtierkunde, Germany. 1104 p.</p><p>Dankwa, H. &amp; Olaosebikan, B.D. 2020. Amphilius atesuensis. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2020.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
